{
  "image_name": "4e69385b25f9c01c7c884e842962a321.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways to harvest electricity by coupling sunlight, heat, and water. At the top, it contrasts “parallel” harvesting, where each energy source independently contributes to electricity, with “serial” harvesting, where one source is converted into another first—for example sunlight producing heat, heat driving evaporation, and water-related processes then generating electricity.\n\nThe middle schematics show device concepts for different pairings. Water plus sunlight appears to use a photovoltaic layer combined with a superhydrophobic surface and electrode under a water droplet. Water plus heat uses a thermoelectric generator (TEG) with a warm side and a water-cooled side. Sunlight plus heat combines a photovoltaic unit stacked on a TEG, exploiting a temperature difference across hot and cold sides.\n\nThe next row illustrates representative mechanisms: heat driving water evaporation and charge generation near a negatively charged surface; sunlight creating a photothermal temperature gradient across a TEG, aided by radiative cooling; and sunlight interacting with water through ion separation across a cation-selective membrane.\n\nAt the bottom, integrated sunlight–heat–water systems are depicted, including condensation/radiative-cooling collection of fresh water and seawater evaporation driven by photothermal layers, suggesting combined water production and power generation.",
